      "explained": {
        "before": "When snapshot assembly ran, a retained refresh was rebuilt from its stored text and accompanying response. That response could come from a later provider read, so capture masking and source metadata could reflect a different read from the text. Cached snapshot output could avoid rebuilding.",
        "after": "When a complete retained source is present, assembly inserts it unchanged and skips rebuilding its capture, title, sections and version. New refreshes and older records without that source still use reconstruction. No recorded proof demonstrates field preservation when assembly runs again.",
        "terms": [
          {
            "term": "kept",
            "meaning": "The optional complete source saved by an earlier refresh."
          },
          {
            "term": "keepFetched",
            "meaning": "The helper that stores a capture after masking text using values from the supplied response."
          },
          {
            "term": "jsonComputation",
            "meaning": "The cached computation boundary that can return an existing snapshot instead of running assembly."
          }
        ]
      },
